Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ArrayBuffer:
  - ToIndex(length) now runs ToPrimitive for a non-numeric argument (an object's
    `valueOf`/`toString`, a string) by routing it through the externref→f64
    coercion chokepoint; a statically numeric argument keeps the direct f64 hint,
    so the common shape is unchanged.
  - ToIndex maps NaN/undefined to 0 and TRUNCATES toward zero. The old
    `len !== floor(len)` test rejected `new ArrayBuffer(1.9)` outright.
  - An unallocatable length is now a catchable RangeError. It used to reach
    `array.new_default` and TRAP ("requested new array is too large"), which no
    `assert.throws` can catch.

DataView, in §25.3.2.1 step order:
  - step 2 brand check, statically for a primitive / foreign-builtin first
    argument and by `ref.test` on the runtime carrier otherwise, raised BEFORE
    ToIndex(byteOffset) so the offset's `valueOf` never runs. The static half
    also fixes two COMPILE_ERRORs: the primitive's f64/i32 result was stored
    into the externref buffer local and the downstream `struct.get` failed Wasm
    validation, killing the whole module.
  - step 7 IsDetachedBuffer → TypeError, after ToIndex and before the bounds
    check (folding it into the offset condition answered RangeError instead).
  - steps 9 and 11.b.ii bounds checks now also apply to the externref-carried
    buffer, which is the common standalone shape.
  - an EXPLICIT `undefined` byteLength means "to the end of the buffer"; `null`
    still coerces to 0.

Neither constructor has a [[Call]] behaviour (§25.1.3.1 / §25.3.2.1 step 1), so
both join the existing call-without-`new` table. Arguments are still evaluated,
never coerced — which is what `DataView/newtarget-undefined-throws.js` asserts.

